1. Confirm that network metrics cover the host interfaces rather than the container’s eth0

The comment in 40-net.conf(https://github.com/open-edge-platform/edge-ai-libraries/blob/1744cc9723592c19c5539f59a4c6db043a2f6a66/microservices/metrics-manager/telegraf.d/40-net.conf#L11)  says “Physical NICs only,” using the interface-name patterns eth*, en*, and wl*.

This excludes names such as docker0, br-, and veth, but the container’s own virtual interface is typically named eth0, which also matches eth*. The filter selects interfaces by name; it does not distinguish physical NICs from virtual ones.

Because the service uses a bridge network in Compose(https://github.com/open-edge-platform/edge-ai-libraries/blob/1744cc9723592c19c5539f59a4c6db043a2f6a66/microservices/metrics-manager/compose.yaml#L186), Telegraf runs in the container’s network namespace. Although “pid: host” is enabled, it shares the PID namespace, not the network namespace. Consequently, /proc/net/dev provides the container’s network counters rather than those of the host’s physical interfaces.

Could you please compare /proc/net/dev on the host and inside the container, then check which interface names appear in the exported net_bytes_* metrics? For example, if the host has enp1s0 but the exported metrics only show the container’s eth0, we are measuring container traffic rather than host NIC traffic. Generating traffic on the host, outside the Metrics Manager container, would also help verify the expected behavior.

If confirmed, please adjust the collection setup so that both net and ethtool monitor the intended host interfaces. A targeted solution would be preferable to switching the entire service to host networking unless necessary.

